  "query_preview": "..High pressure remains centered near the Four Corners\nregion this evening. While westerly flow aloft is in place across\nthe northern half of Utah, southerly flow across northern AZ and\nsouthern NV continue to facilitate the flow of moisture into the\narea. Latest analysis shows generally between 0.8-1.1 inches of\nPWAT across the forecast area. Isolated to widely scattered \nshowers and isolated thunderstorms will continue into the late \nevening hours before diminishing overnight. \n\nFor Tuesday, as moisture remains in place, expect scattered \nconvection to develop again in the afternoon. Initial rounds of \nconvection look to be tied to the higher terrain, then models \nsuggest that remnants of the MCV currently over southern AZ could \npotentially rotate around the periphery of the high and lift north\nthrough portions of western Utah during the late afternoon \nthrough evening hours, then across northern Utah overnight. \nShowers and thunderstorms will be accompanied by the typical \nthreats of flash flooding and gusty outflow winds. \n\nThe ridge axis is progged to shift east for the middle to latter\npart of the week. This will bring more of a southwesterly flow to\nthe area which will start spreading some drier air into the area,\nwith PWATs gradually decreasing, especially Thursday into the \nweekend. However, a shortwave trough which is progged to eject \nacross northwest Utah on Thursday will provide sufficient lift\nthat will interact with lingering low-level moisture to result in\nmore widespread convection, with some added shear to result in \npotentially stronger storms. \n\nModels then hint at the drying trend continuing into next week as\nthe ridge axis shifts over Utah. This is supported by various EC \nand CMC ensemble members, although the GFS keeps the ridge axis\nfarther east with higher PWATs remaining in place over Utah.",
